A Mathematical Framework for Collective Intelligence Architecture in Closed-Habitat Human–AI Systems

Ouadi Maakoul

AI: Claude, Gemini, Deepseek 

---

Abstract

This dissertation develops a rigorous mathematical foundation for the design and governance of collective intelligence in closed-habitat human–AI societies, motivated by the survival requirement that no critical knowledge domain be extinguished through attrition or network fragmentation. We depart from single-layer graph models and scalar knowledge inventories, replacing them with a multilayer tensor formalism, knowledge vectors in a reproducing kernel Hilbert space, and spectral analysis of the colony’s communication Laplacian. Four principal contributions are advanced. First, we define the multiplex cognitive network and derive inter-layer coupling conditions that govern cascade failures. Second, we embed agent competencies in a Hilbert space, recasting redundancy and diversity as geometric spanning problems. Third, we establish the Spectral Governance Lemma, linking the algebraic connectivity of the knowledge-transfer graph to an exponential bound on global knowledge-extinction probability. Fourth, we supply finite-size percolation corrections—scaling ansatz, modularity penalty, and extreme-value fragmentation times—that adapt asymptotic results to colonies of 50 to 1000 members. These results are integrated into an operational architecture in which the Collective Intelligence Architect acts as a Spectral Governor, continuously monitoring eigenvalue spectra and structural divergence from a safe reference topology, and exercising mathematically grounded veto authority over personnel rotations or AI integrations that would push the system outside its stable manifold.

