1. Introduction

Closed-habitat communities—lunar settlements, long-duration space missions, submarine crews—confront a lethal problem: the irreversible loss of survival-critical knowledge through death, rotation, or reassignment. Empirical organisational research demonstrates that knowledge loss concentrates on employees who occupy structural holes; their departure fragments communication networks and silos essential competences. Simultaneously, the integration of artificial intelligence into decision pipelines introduces a fragility distinct from simple human attrition: AI-mediated compression can silently attenuate the information available for sensemaking, amplifying errors instead of correcting them.

Prior attempts to formalise collective intelligence have modelled groups as single-layer networks and measured knowledge as a scalar mastery level. These approximations become dangerous at the scale of a small colony, where the sharp phase transitions of infinite-network percolation theory do not materialise and where human relationships, trust delegations, and AI-access pathways form overlapping but topologically distinct layers. A mathematically adequate architecture must (i) represent the multiplex nature of the socio-cognitive fabric, (ii) quantify knowledge geometrically to expose hidden overlaps and critical single-point-of-failure vectors, (iii) govern the network’s spectral properties rather than superficial connectivity, and (iv) correct all percolation thresholds for finite population size.

This dissertation constructs that architecture. We supply the formal definitions, prove the core stability theorems, develop the finite-size correction apparatus, and translate the mathematics into an actionable job description for the Collective Intelligence Architect—a role we demonstrate must exist as a distinct, spectrally governed safety authority.

