2. The Multiplex Cognitive Network and Knowledge Hilbert Space

2.1 Formal System Definition

Definition 2.1 (Multiplex Cognitive Network).
A lunar cognitive system is the multiplex graph

\mathcal{M} = (V, \mathcal{E}_1, \mathcal{E}_2, \mathcal{E}_3, \mathcal{K})

where:

·  V = V_H \cup V_A  is the set of human and AI agents,  |V| = N .
·  \mathcal{E}_1 \subseteq V \times V  is the communication layer (undirected, weighted by latency and bandwidth).
·  \mathcal{E}_2 \subseteq V \times V  is the cognitive delegation layer (directed; a directed edge  u \to v  indicates that  u  delegates processing of a domain to  v ).
·  \mathcal{E}_3 \subseteq V \times \mathcal{K}  is the knowledge-access layer, a bipartite graph linking agents to competency nodes  k \in \mathcal{K} .
·  \mathcal{K}  is the set of survival-critical knowledge domains.

The state of the multiplex is captured by the adjacency tensor  \mathcal{A} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N \times 3}  together with the bipartite assignment matrix  B \in \{0,1\}^{N \times |\mathcal{K}|} .

Definition 2.2 (Inter-Layer Coupling).
A cascade from layer  \alpha  to layer  \beta  occurs if the removal of edges in layer  \alpha  increases the path length in layer  \beta  beyond a critical threshold. For a multiplex, the multiplex path length between two nodes is the minimum over all sequences of intra- and inter-layer steps; we formalise this using a supra-adjacency matrix  \mathbf{S}  of dimension  N \times 3 . The coupling strength is measured by the sensitivity of the algebraic connectivity of the supra-Laplacian to edge removal in a single layer.

2.2 Knowledge as a Hilbert Space Vector

We replace the scalar mastery  \Phi(v,k)  with a vector representation that captures both breadth and depth and that permits rigorous orthogonality-based redundancy measures.

Let  \mathcal{H}  be a real reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS) of functions over the domain of competencies. Each agent  v  is assigned a knowledge vector  \mathbf{k}_v \in \mathcal{H} . The RKHS structure supplies an inner product  \langle \cdot , \cdot \rangle_{\mathcal{H}}  and a positive-definite kernel  \kappa(k_i, k_j) = \langle \phi(k_i), \phi(k_j) \rangle_{\mathcal{H}}  where  \phi  is a feature map. The normalised inner product

\rho(v_i, v_j) = \frac{\langle \mathbf{k}_{v_i}, \mathbf{k}_{v_j} \rangle_{\mathcal{H}}}{\|\mathbf{k}_{v_i}\|_{\mathcal{H}} \|\mathbf{k}_{v_j}\|_{\mathcal{H}}}

quantifies knowledge similarity:  \rho \approx 1  signals high redundancy (identical expertise), while  \rho \approx 0  signals maximal diversity.

Definition 2.3 (Colony Knowledge Gramian).
The Gram matrix  G \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N}  with entries  G_{ij} = \langle \mathbf{k}_{v_i}, \mathbf{k}_{v_j} \rangle_{\mathcal{H}}  encodes the pairwise knowledge overlaps of the population. The effective functional redundancy for a target domain represented by a reference vector  \mathbf{f} \in \mathcal{H}  is the squared norm of the projection of  \mathbf{f}  onto the span of the agent vectors:

R_{\text{eff}}(\mathbf{f}) = \mathbf{f}^\top \mathbf{K} (\mathbf{K}^\top \mathbf{K})^{-1} \mathbf{K}^\top \mathbf{f},

where  \mathbf{K}  is the matrix whose columns are  \mathbf{k}_{v_i} . The colony possesses complete coverage when  \operatorname{span}(\{\mathbf{k}_v\})  contains the functional requirement subspace  \mathcal{F} \subset \mathcal{H} .

Theorem 2.4 (Geometric Optimal Redundancy).
Let  \mathcal{F} \subseteq \mathcal{H}  be the functional requirement subspace of dimension  d . The minimum number of agents needed to cover  \mathcal{F}  with redundancy  r  (i.e., every direction is spanned by at least  r  vectors) is the solution to a basis pursuit problem:

\min_{V' \subseteq V} |V'| \quad \text{subject to} \quad \operatorname{span}(\{\mathbf{k}_v\}_{v \in V'}) \supseteq \mathcal{F} \text{ and } \forall \mathbf{f} \in \mathcal{F}, \sum_{v} \langle \mathbf{k}_v, \mathbf{f} \rangle^2 \geq r \|\mathbf{f}\|^2.

This formulation eliminates intuitive “headcount” redundancy and replaces it with a precisely computable geometric condition.

