3. Spectral Governance and Stability Invariants

3.1 Laplacian and Adjacency Spectra

The communication and delegation layers jointly determine the speed and reliability of knowledge flow. We model the combined effective topology by a weighted undirected graph with adjacency matrix  W  constructed from  \mathcal{E}_1  and the symmetrised trust weights of  \mathcal{E}_2 . The normalised Laplacian is

\mathcal{L} = I - D^{-1/2} W D^{-1/2},

with spectrum  0 = \lambda_1 \le \lambda_2 \le \dots \le \lambda_N \le 2 . The spectral radius of the (directed) delegation layer is denoted  \rho(A) .

Definition 3.1 (Spectral Envelope).
The stable operating region for a colony of size  N  is the set

\Lambda_{\text{safe}} = \left\{ (\lambda_2, \rho(A), \gamma) \; \middle| \; \lambda_2 > \epsilon_{\text{crit}},\; \rho(A) < \rho_{\text{threshold}},\; \gamma = \lambda_3 - \lambda_2 > \delta_{\text{min}} \right\},

where  \epsilon_{\text{crit}}, \rho_{\text{threshold}}, \delta_{\text{min}}  are mission-specific constants derived from the maximum tolerable fragmentation risk, AI error propagation speed, and community isolation tolerance.

3.2 The Spectral Governance Lemma

Lemma 3.2 (Algebraic Connectivity and Knowledge Extinction).
Assume knowledge items diffuse across the network with rate  \lambda_{\text{max}}  and node (agent) failure occurs independently at rate  \mu . If the colony’s knowledge coverage is maintained by a process that repairs lost knowledge at a rate proportional to the algebraic connectivity  \lambda_2 , then the probability  P_{\text{ext}}  that any critical domain falls entirely out of the agent population satisfies

P_{\text{ext}} \le \exp\!\left( -\alpha \left( \lambda_2 - \frac{\mu}{\lambda_{\text{max}}} \right) N \right)

for some constant  \alpha > 0 . Consequently, a sufficient condition for exponentially small extinction risk is

\lambda_2 > \frac{\mu}{\lambda_{\text{max}}}.

Proof sketch. Model the presence of domain  k  as a susceptible–infected–susceptible (SIS) process on the network, where “infected” means an agent possesses the knowledge. The epidemic threshold for SIS on a network is given by  \tau = 1/\rho(A) , but for our controlled diffusion (where we actively push knowledge via designed edges) the relevant quantity is the spectral gap. Using a mean-field bound and the fact that the mixing time of the random walk on the graph scales as  1/\lambda_2 , the time to reach an absorbing state where all copies of a domain are lost grows exponentially with  (\lambda_2 - \mu/\lambda_{\text{max}}) N . A rigorous proof applies the Chernoff bound for expander graphs.  \blacksquare 

This lemma furnishes the fundamental design inequality that the Collective Intelligence Architect must enforce at all times.

3.3 Spectral Radius and Error Propagation

The directed delegation layer governs how an AI-generated hallucination or a human cognitive error propagates. The spectral radius  \rho(A)  is the asymptotic per-step amplification factor.

Theorem 3.3 (Error Amplification Bound).
Let  \mathbf{e}^{(0)}  be an initial error vector injected at a set of nodes, and let  \mathbf{e}^{(t)} = A^t \mathbf{e}^{(0)}  describe the linearised propagation of misinformation through the delegation network. Then

\|\mathbf{e}^{(t)}\|_2 \le \rho(A)^t \|\mathbf{e}^{(0)}\|_2.

If  \rho(A) > 1 , even arbitrarily small errors eventually dominate the system. To prevent this, the CIA must enforce  \rho(A) \le \rho_{\text{threshold}} < 1 / \beta , where  \beta  is a safety factor incorporating non-linear saturation.

This directly anchors the fragility propagation of Theorem 4 (information-theoretic) in the network’s topology, making it an eigenvalue constraint.

