5. Fragility Propagation in Human–AI Pipelines

5.1 Information-Theoretic Formulation

Consider a processing chain:

X_0 \xrightarrow{\text{acquisition}} X_1 \xrightarrow{\text{AI compression}} X_2 \xrightarrow{\text{human decision}} Y,

where  X_0  is the true world state,  X_1  raw sensor data,  X_2  an AI-produced summary, and  Y  a decision. The data-processing inequality yields

I(Y; X_0) \le I(X_2; X_1) \le I(X_1; X_0).

Theorem 5.1 (Non-Attenuating Distortion).
Suppose the AI compression step implements a deterministic mapping  f: \mathcal{X}_1 \to \mathcal{X}_2  that is not injective. Then there exists an error signal  \epsilon  in  X_1  such that the decision-stage distortion, measured by expected 0–1 loss  L , satisfies  L(Y, Y^*) \ge L(X_1, X_0)  for some configuration of states. In particular, compression cannot decrease the minimum probability of error.

Proof. By the data-processing inequality, the Bayes error is monotone non-decreasing under any deterministic transformation. Because  f  is non-injective, there exist  x_1 \neq x_1'  with  f(x_1) = f(x_1') ; hence the decision stage cannot distinguish them, and an error present in  X_1  survives or is magnified.  \blacksquare 

Corollary 5.2 (Architectural Requirement).
Any AI system placed between raw observations and life-support decisions must expose its compression losses. Acceptable interfaces preserve the uncertainty structure—e.g., by providing full posterior distributions or audit-log traces—so that the mutual information  I(X_2; X_0)  does not fall below a safety floor. This requirement becomes a binding element of the CIA’s audit authority.

