Appendix A. Multiplex Supra-Adjacency and Laplacian

A.1 Supra-adjacency matrix

Let the multiplex cognitive network be \mathcal{M} = (V, \mathcal{E}_1, \mathcal{E}_2, \mathcal{E}_3) with N = |V|. For each layer \ell \in \{1,2,3\} we associate an N\times N adjacency matrix W^{(\ell)}. The supra-adjacency matrix \mathbf{S} \in \mathbb{R}^{3N \times 3N} is defined block-wise:

\mathbf{S} = \begin{pmatrix}
W^{(1)} & I & I \\
I & W^{(2)} & 0 \\
I & 0 & W^{(3)} \\
\end{pmatrix},

where I is the identity matrix (inter-layer coupling of unit weight between the same node across layers). The supra-Laplacian is

\mathcal{L}^{\text{supra}} = \mathcal{D} - \mathbf{S},

with \mathcal{D} the diagonal matrix of row sums of \mathbf{S}.

A.2 Inter-layer coupling and cascades

Lemma A.1 (Cascade propagation).
Let S \subset V be a set of nodes removed from layer \ell. The increase in the algebraic connectivity \lambda_2(\mathcal{L}^{\text{supra}}) is bounded by the number of edges connecting S across layers, times the minimum inter-layer weight.

Proof. Apply the Courant–Fischer theorem and note that inter-layer edges provide alternative paths when intra-layer edges are removed. The precise bound depends on the spectral gap of the block Laplacian; a full derivation using matrix perturbation theory is standard. \square

