Appendix B. Knowledge Hilbert Space and Gramian Redundancy

B.1 Reproducing kernel Hilbert space formulation

Let \mathcal{K} be the set of knowledge domains. Choose a positive-definite kernel \kappa: \mathcal{K}\times \mathcal{K} \to \mathbb{R}, e.g., \kappa(k,k') = \exp(-\gamma \|k-k'\|^2) after embedding domains in a metric space. The RKHS \mathcal{H} is the completion of the linear span of \{\kappa(\cdot, k) : k \in \mathcal{K}\}. For an agent v, the knowledge vector \mathbf{k}_v \in \mathcal{H} is defined by its evaluation functional: f(k) = \langle f, \kappa(\cdot,k) \rangle_{\mathcal{H}}. We identify \mathbf{k}_v with the function k \mapsto \Phi(v,k) (mastery level), ensuring \mathbf{k}_v is the unique representer.

B.2 Effective redundancy and spanning

Definition B.1 (Gramian and projection). For a population V with vectors \{\mathbf{k}_v\}, the Gram matrix is G_{ij} = \langle \mathbf{k}_{v_i}, \mathbf{k}_{v_j} \rangle_{\mathcal{H}}. The effective redundancy of a functional requirement \mathbf{f} \in \mathcal{H} is

R_{\text{eff}}(\mathbf{f}) = \mathbf{f}^\top \mathbf{K} (\mathbf{K}^\top \mathbf{K})^{-1} \mathbf{K}^\top \mathbf{f},

where \mathbf{K} is the matrix whose columns are the coefficient vectors of \mathbf{k}_v with respect to an orthonormal basis.

Theorem B.2 (Minimum agents for \epsilon-coverage).
Given a finite-dimensional subspace \mathcal{F} \subset \mathcal{H} of dimension d, the minimum number of agents needed so that every \mathbf{f} \in \mathcal{F} satisfies R_{\text{eff}}(\mathbf{f}) \ge 1-\epsilon is the solution to

\min_{V' \subseteq V} |V'| \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \sigma_{\min}(\mathbf{K}_{V'}^\top) \ge \sqrt{1-\epsilon},

where \mathbf{K}_{V'} denotes the restriction of \mathbf{K} to agents in V', and \sigma_{\min} is the smallest singular value. The problem is a combinatorial constraint; greedy selection by maximizing the projection norm (matching pursuit) yields a logarithmic approximation.

Proof. The condition R_{\text{eff}}(\mathbf{f}) \ge 1-\epsilon for all unit-norm \mathbf{f} is equivalent to \|P_{\text{span}(\mathbf{K}_{V'})} \mathbf{f}\|^2 \ge 1-\epsilon. This holds iff the smallest singular value of the projection matrix is at least \sqrt{1-\epsilon}. The greedy algorithm proof follows standard results on column subset selection. \square

