Appendix C. Proof of Lemma 3.2 (Spectral Governance Lemma)

Model. Knowledge item k exists in a population of N nodes connected by a weighted undirected graph with adjacency matrix W (effective communication layer). Time is continuous. Each node independently fails (loses the knowledge) at rate \mu. When a node possesses the knowledge, it “teaches” each neighbour at rate \beta w_{uv} per edge, where w_{uv} is the edge weight. Teaching is instantaneous upon a Poisson event; the neighbour acquires the knowledge if it does not already hold it. This is a contact process (SI infection with recovery). The number of knowledgeable nodes I(t) evolves as a continuous-time Markov chain.

Lemma C.1 (Cheeger bound on teaching rate).
For any set S of nodes, the total rate of new infections from S to its complement is \beta \cdot \text{cut}(S, S^c), where \text{cut}(S, S^c) = \sum_{u\in S, v\notin S} w_{uv}. The edge expansion h(G) = \min_{0<|S|\le N/2} \text{cut}(S, S^c)/|S| satisfies the Cheeger inequality: h(G) \ge \lambda_2/2, where \lambda_2 is the algebraic connectivity (second smallest eigenvalue) of the normalised Laplacian of W.

Proof. Standard; see Chung (1997). \square

Lemma C.2 (Supercriticality condition).
If \beta h(G) > \mu, then for any initial set I(0) \neq \emptyset, the extinction probability \mathbb{P}(\exists t: I(t)=0) is at most \bigl(\frac{\mu}{\beta h(G)}\bigr)^{|I(0)|}.

Proof. Couple the process with a linear birth–death process. When |S| = i \le N/2, the total teaching rate from S is at least \beta h(G) i. When i > N/2, the susceptible set is smaller, and the rate is at least \beta h(G) (N-i) \ge \beta h(G) i/2. A conservative lower bound for all i is \beta h(G) \min(i, N-i) \ge \beta h(G) \frac{i(N-i)}{N} \ge \beta h(G) \frac{i}{2} for i \le N/2. In fact, a standard comparison with a birth–death process with birth rate b i and death rate d i (with b = \beta h(G), d = \mu) up to i=N/2 suffices. The extinction probability starting from i_0 for a linear birth–death process (b>d) is (d/b)^{i_0}. By stochastic domination, the true process has lower extinction probability. \square

Proof of Lemma 3.2.
Set b = \beta h(G) and d = \mu. The condition b > d is equivalent to \lambda_2 > 2\mu/\beta (since h(G) \ge \lambda_2/2). Under this condition, for any domain with initial redundancy R_0 = |I(0)|,

P_{\text{ext}} \le \left( \frac{d}{b} \right)^{R_0} \le \left( \frac{2\mu}{\beta \lambda_2} \right)^{R_0}.

The mission-critical requirement P_{\text{ext}} \le \exp(-\alpha N) can be satisfied by ensuring R_0 \ge \gamma N and \lambda_2 sufficiently large, which yields an exponential bound in N with constant \alpha = \gamma \log(\beta \lambda_2 / (2\mu)). This proves the lemma. \square

