Appendix E. Finite-Size Percolation Corrections

E.1 Scaling ansatz

Standard percolation theory on an infinite graph gives a sharp threshold p_c(\infty). For a finite system of size N, the fraction of vertices in the giant component P_\infty(p,N) obeys the finite-size scaling form

P_\infty(p,N) = N^{-\beta/\nu} \mathcal{F}\big( (p - p_c(\infty)) N^{1/\nu} \big),

where \beta and \nu are critical exponents. The effective threshold p_c(N) can be defined as the point where P_\infty = 1/2. Shifting the scaling variable yields

p_c(N) = p_c(\infty) + A N^{-1/\nu},

with A = \mathcal{F}^{-1}(1/2). For networks in the mean-field universality class, \nu = 3; for scale-free graphs, exponents may differ. The constant A must be estimated from simulation.

E.2 Modularity penalty

Real small-group networks exhibit clustering. We use the generating function formalism for clustered networks (Newman, 2009). Let the joint degree distribution be p_{st}, where s is the number of single edges and t the number of triangle edges. The effective percolation threshold is shifted by the clustering coefficient \mathcal{C}. A first-order approximation gives

p_c^{\text{eff}} = p_c(\infty) + \Delta p_c(N) + \Omega \cdot \mathcal{C},

where \Omega depends on the second moments of the degree distribution and can be derived from the generating function. A full derivation is lengthy but standard; we omit the algebra and state the result. \square

