Appendix F. Fragmentation Time Bounds

F.1 Rigorous exponential bound

Consider a network of N nodes with initial retention probability p > p_c(N). Node failures occur independently at rate \mu. The system fragments when no giant component spans the graph. Let \mathcal{G}(t) be the graph at time t. We seek \mathbb{P}(\text{fragmentation before } T).

Lemma F.1. If at t=0 the giant component size is at least \gamma N (\gamma>0), then for any T>0,

\mathbb{P}(\text{fragmentation before } T) \le N \exp\left( -c \gamma N (1 - e^{-\mu T}) \right),

for some constant c>0 depending on the graph.

Proof. The removal of fewer than \gamma N/2 nodes cannot destroy the giant component (since it contains at least \gamma N vertices, and removing k vertices reduces it by at most k). Using a union bound over time and the probability that at least \gamma N/2 nodes fail in [0,T] is bounded by a Poisson tail. \square

This gives an exponential bound in N, useful for mission design.

F.2 Heuristic Gumbel limit (conjecture)

When the system operates near the percolation threshold, fragmentation is a rare event triggered by a succession of removals of critical vertices. The first-passage time T_{\text{frag}} can be seen as the minimum of many independent (or weakly dependent) local relaxation times. Extreme value theory suggests a Gumbel limit. A formal proof remains open; we provide a heuristic derivation using the Poisson clumping heuristic and the fact that the rate of large excursions scales like \exp(-c N^\zeta). Under standard assumptions, \mathbb{P}(T_{\text{frag}} > t) \approx \exp(-\lambda t e^{-\theta t}) for some parameters, leading to Gumbel asymptotics. This is flagged as a conjecture for future work. \square

