Appendix H. Proof of Theorem 5.1 and Corollary 5.2 (Information-Theoretic Fragility)

Theorem 5.1 (Non-attenuating distortion). Consider a processing chain X_0 \to X_1 \to X_2 \to Y, where X_2 = f(X_1) with f a deterministic, non-injective mapping. Let P_e = \min_{g} \mathbb{P}(g(X_2) \neq X_0) be the minimum probability of error for recovering X_0 from X_2, and similarly P_e^{(1)} = \min_{h} \mathbb{P}(h(X_1) \neq X_0). Then P_e \ge P_e^{(1)}.

Proof. The data-processing inequality for mutual information gives I(X_0; X_2) \le I(X_0; X_1). Fano’s inequality states that for any estimator \hat{X}_0(Y),

\mathbb{P}(\hat{X}_0 \neq X_0) \ge \frac{H(X_0) - I(X_0; Y) - 1}{\log |\mathcal{X}_0|}.

Minimising over decision rules preserves the inequality, so P_e \ge \frac{H(X_0)-I(X_0;X_2)-1}{\log |\mathcal{X}_0|} \ge \frac{H(X_0)-I(X_0;X_1)-1}{\log |\mathcal{X}_0|} \ge P_e^{(1)}. Therefore compression never reduces the Bayes error. Moreover, non-injectivity ensures strict inequality for some distributions. \square

Corollary 5.2 (Architectural requirement). To keep P_e below a safety threshold, the mutual information I(X_0; X_2) must be bounded below. This forces the AI interface to be information-lossless within the required fidelity, i.e., it must preserve a posterior distribution or full audit trail. \square

